2.1 What is Inheritance?
`Definition`

Inheritance is a mechanism in Java through which one class acquires
properties and behaviors of another class.

The existing class is called the parent/superclass, and the new class
is called the child/subclass.


Example:

class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal eats");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

Animal is the parent class and Dog is the child class.

Relationship:
Animal
   ↑
   |
  Dog

The child gets accessible members from the parent according to Java's
inheritance and access-control rules.

Here:
class Dog extends Animal

means:
Dog inherits from Animal.

Using it:
class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog();
        d.eat();
        d.bark();
    }
}

Output:
Animal eats
Dog barks

Dog can use:
bark()

because it declares it itself.

It can also use:
eat()
because it inherits that method from Animal.



2.3 extends Keyword

The extends keyword is used to create class inheritance.

class Parent {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Child");
    }
}



Now Child inherits from Parent.
Child c = new Child();

c.show();
c.display();

Output:
Parent
Child





2.4 Why Do We Need Inheritance?

One major reason is code reuse.

Suppose:
class Dog {

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }

    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking");
    }
}

and:
class Cat {

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }

    void meow() {
        System.out.println("Meowing");
    }
}

The eat() method is duplicated.

With inheritance:

class Animal {

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking");
    }
}

class Cat extends Animal {

    void meow() {
        System.out.println("Meowing");
    }
}

Now eat() is defined once in Animal and inherited by both Dog and Cat.




2.4 Types of Inheritance in Java

For classes, the important forms are:

1. Single inheritance
One parent → one child.

Animal
   |
  Dog

class Animal {
}

class Dog extends Animal {
}



2. Multilevel inheritance

Parent → child → grandchild.

Animal
   |
  Dog
   |
 Puppy

class Animal {
}

class Dog extends Animal {
}

class Puppy extends Dog {
}

Puppy inherits through the chain.


3. Hierarchical inheritance

One parent → multiple children.
       Animal
       /    \
     Dog    Cat


class Animal {
}

class Dog extends Animal {
}

class Cat extends Animal {
}


Both Dog and Cat inherit from Animal.




2.6 Multiple Inheritance Through Classes

Java "does not support multiple" inheritance of classes.

This is not allowed:
class A {
}

class B {
}

// Not allowed
class C extends A, B {
}


The problem is ambiguity.

Suppose:
class A {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("A");
    }
}

class B {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("B");
    }
}

If Java allowed:
class C extends A, B {
}

then:
C c = new C();

c.show();

Which show() should execute?
A? or B?


This kind of ambiguity is one reason Java does not allow multiple inheritance
through classes.

Java can achieve multiple inheritance of type/contract through interfaces,
which we will cover later under Abstraction.



2.7 super Keyword

super is used in a child class to refer to the immediate parent class.
It can be used to:

1. Access parent class variables.
2. Call parent class methods.
3. Call parent class constructors.
A. super for Parent Variable


class Parent {
    String name = "Parent";
}
class Child extends Parent {
    String name = "Child";
    void display() {
        System.out.println(name);
        System.out.println(super.name);
    }
}


Using:
Child c = new Child();
c.display();

Output:
Child
Parent

Here:
name

refers to the child variable.
super.name
refers to the parent variable.


2.8 super for Parent Method
class Parent {
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Parent method");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Child method");
    }

    void show() {
        display();
        super.display();
    }
}

Using:
Child c = new Child();

c.show();

Output:
Child method
Parent method


Here:
display();

calls the child version.
super.display();
explicitly calls the parent version.



2.9 Constructor Execution in Inheritance

This is very important.

When a child object is created, the parent constructor executes before the
child constructor.

Example:
class Parent {
    Parent() {
        System.out.println("Parent Constructor");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    Child() {
        System.out.println("Child Constructor");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Child c = new Child();
    }
}

Output:
Parent Constructor
Child Constructor

Why?
When:
new Child();
is executed, Java must initialize the inherited parent portion before
completing child initialization.

Conceptually:
new Child()
    ↓
Parent constructor
    ↓
Child constructor
    ↓
Child object ready